window.GUIDE_INDEX = {"locale":"en","words":{"ph":"Search the guide","no":"Nothing found","n":"{n} results","n1":"1 result","hint":"Press / to search"},"sections":[{"p":"accounts.html","a":"","t":"Safety & fixes","u":"","b":"Safety & fixes What your passphrase protects, what Daimond asks before it acts, what stays private, and what to do when something is wrong."},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s1","t":"Your account is a passphrase","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"On first run Daimond generates a passphrase of eight random words and shows it once. It is the key to everything stored here. It is not sent anywhere, it is not stored in the clear, and nobody can reset it. It unlocks everything encrypted here, meaning your provider keys, your mail credentials and the account's own signing key, and the key that seals the sync parcel is derived from it too. The signing key is what signs you in for credits. Forget the passphrase and the encrypted data is gone. Once a passphrase is in place you can add a passkey for a faster unlock, by face, fingerprint or security key. It unseals the passphrase for you instead of replacing it, so the passphrase stays the fallback. The unlock happens on the device, and the credits service only ever sees a public key."},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s2","t":"The account controls","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"Your name is at the top of the account area, at the foot of the rail. Change name&hellip; and Change passphrase&hellip; do what they say, and the second re-wraps your encrypted data under the new passphrase. Add a passkey&hellip; Export a backup writes your chats, Diamonds, workspace files and your account key to a file you keep. Import a backup&hellip; adopts the backup's account where this browser holds none, and where one is already here it brings back only the work. Log out locks the app. Your data stays on the device, encrypted. Forget this identity&hellip; removes the account and its encrypted data from this browser for good. Beside them, Syncing turns this device's sync on or off, and Diagnostics shows and copies the app's own log, which holds event names and a clock, with no keys, no message text and nothing from your files. That log is what to paste into a report."},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s3","t":"Moving your account","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"Your account is a signing key held in this browser. The passphrase does not recreate it , and only decrypts the copy stored here. So the same passphrase in a fresh browser starts a separate account, with its own credits and no Pro. The key itself has to travel, and three things carry it. Link another device , which shows a pairing code. A passkey, which stands a new device up in one gesture. Export a backup , whose file holds the key for Import a backup&hellip; to adopt in a browser that has none. Do one of them before you retire a browser. Once its storage is gone, with no backup taken and no other device linked, the key is gone with it. A backup holds your work in the clear and your key wrapped under your passphrase. Importing the file alone makes nobody you. Whoever holds the file and the passphrase holds the account, which is why the two should not travel together."},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s4","t":"More than one person on one browser","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"One browser can hold several accounts, each with its own chats, keys, credits and files. ＋ Add another account makes a second, and the row above it switches between them. Switching locks the first and forgets its keys before opening the other. Nobody sees another account's data."},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s5","t":"What Daimond asks before it acts","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"A chip beside the model says what Daimond does without asking. It has three settings, and they apply to the whole app. Ask every time reads Every command is put to you first. So is reaching the web, once in each conversation. Guarded reads Commands run. Once something written by somebody else has been read, Daimond asks before reaching out. It is the default. Bypass reads Nothing is asked, whatever has been read. Under Guarded, once a turn has read something written by somebody else, such as a web page, an email or a build log, anything it then wants to fetch or send is put to you first, and a command in that turn runs with the network refused. That is the case where text you did not write could talk Daimond into carrying your work somewhere. Switching to Bypass asks once, and says what you give up. No mode moves the fence a command runs inside, a Diamond's folders, the system-call filter, or the log you can read afterwards. An agent working on its own is asked about separately. Clicking something on a page can spend money or submit a form, and none of that can be taken back, so a click by an unattended agent is put to you even under looser settings. Money has its own brakes, and t"},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s6","t":"What stays private","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"Your passphrase, provider keys, mail credentials, chats, Diamonds and files live in this browser and are not sent to us. What we hold is money and ciphertext, meaning your balance and licences and, for sync, a parcel we cannot open. Your messages reach a model provider directly with your own key, or through our metered service when you spend credits. Fetching a page, and syncing or sending mail, pass through the gateway. So the honest claim is that with your own key nothing of your work reaches us in the clear, and that you can watch only ciphertext leave for our servers. You need not take that on trust. The code running in this browser is public, it builds byte for byte from that source, and every build served is sealed into a published record. Verify this build , in the About dialog, checks the one you are running. Open your browser's network panel and watch where the calls go. The protection has limits. A passphrase guards against someone opening the browser's developer tools, and not against a compromised browser, a malicious extension or a keylogger, which defeat any in-browser scheme. And what a remote server does inside itself cannot be proven from outside, which is why it i"},{"p":"accounts.html","a":"s7","t":"When something goes wrong","u":"Safety & fixes","b":"Connect a model to start. No model is connected. Add one, or add a key for the provider already listed. Your second device is empty. You made a second account instead of pairing. Pair it, or import a backup. See More than one device . A shared folder is not arriving. It is over the 200 MiB a shared folder may weigh. A banner names it. Share a smaller folder. Not valid JSON. Nothing saved. A hand edit to a Diamond's memory left the file unreadable, so nothing was written. Fix the text or close without saving. Nothing runs on its own. The light is red because the action is stopped. Press play on the light beside it. Terminal needs the Daimond Hand on this computer. Install&hellip; beside it goes to Your computer . A turn is sitting on another device. That machine is probably waiting for a folder permission on its own screen. Go and look, or press Run here . A panel has disappeared. It is unpinned, and still there. The ⋯ chip opens the gallery, and Ctrl K reaches any panel by name. Everything is locked after a reload. The key is held in memory and nowhere else, unless this device is set to stay unlocked across reloads. Enter the passphrase, or use a passkey. Still stuck? The rail hold"},{"p":"capps.html","a":"","t":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","u":"","b":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page Every Diamond is drawn by a page of its own. By default that page renders what the Diamond knows. Ask, and it becomes a small application, something you tap that records what you tapped. The page is a real file, crystal.html , in the Diamond's own folder. Every Diamond has one from the day it is made, and the same rules cover the plain one and the elaborate one."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s1","t":"Asking for one","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"Open the Diamond and choose Redesign page&hellip; in the row above the crystal. The message box fills with one line, Change how this page looks: , with the caret at the end of it. Finish the sentence and send. The rules the page must obey ride with that turn and are never shown to you. They are the app's contract with the page, identical every time, and you cannot reword them. Delete the opening line and you are asking something else, so they do not ride. Say what you tap and what it should record. \"A tile for each of the foods I eat: tapping one records it with today's date, and the day's total is at the top.\" A daimon cannot guess whether an entry is a count, a weight or a time, so say which."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s2","t":"What the page can do","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"Draw what the Diamond knows. Everything is handed to it on load, and again whenever you change how Daimond looks, so it can dress itself to match. Read the files in its own Diamond. A table it charts, an index it maintains, yesterday's log. Write files there. This is what makes it an application. A tap is recorded, and it is still there tomorrow. Ask Daimond to open a link , through the same gate every other outward request goes through. Writing appends by default, so two taps in the same instant both survive. One write is capped at half a megabyte, and the file itself has no ceiling and grows a line at a time."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s3","t":"What it cannot do","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"The page runs in a sealed frame, and none of the limits here is a setting. The page was written by a model, it is not part of what the Diamond knows, and it travels to every device you have linked. No network. No fetch of any kind and no image from a server, so it cannot call an API and cannot send anywhere what you put in it. No libraries. One self-contained file, with pictures drawn or inlined and code of its own. It cannot build fresh code out of text while it runs. 512 KB for the whole page. Page size limit in Settings raises it. No storage of its own. Nothing survives between loads except the files it asks Daimond to write, which you can read. It cannot rewrite itself , nor the memory, nor the version history. It cannot leave its Diamond. Every path resolves inside that one folder, so one page cannot read another Diamond's files, and none of them reaches your workspace. What the page gives up, and what it gets. Such a page is plainer than the little apps other tools write for you, and it reaches another person only when you share its Diamond. In exchange it works with no connection, what is in it stays on your devices unless you share it or a daimon reads it, and no subscripti"},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s4","t":"Where what you record goes","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"It goes into the Diamond, beside the page, in the browser's own storage. That storage is on your machine, but it is not a folder you can open in a file manager, and it is not the folder you have open as your workspace. You read it in the Workspace panel. Deleting the page does not delete what it recorded. The files stay, and a new page can read them. The files travel with the Diamond, so a log kept on your phone is on your laptop. Two things follow. A Diamond travels whole , from whichever device touched it last. Log on two devices between two syncs and the later one wins, so log in one place at a time. Bulk data competes. Diamonds share a few megabytes in a parcel, and one that outgrows what is left is left out of the sync instead of being trimmed. A log of what you did stays small for years, while a reference table of tens of megabytes belongs in the workspace."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s5","t":"Changing it, and putting it back","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"Ask for changes the same way. Every version of the page is kept, History lists them, and any can be restored. A page that will not load is not a dead end. Daimond puts its own rendering up instead, says which way it failed, and offers to put the standard page back."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s6","t":"On a phone","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"At the right-hand end of the row above the page is a button drawn as four corners, Full screen . It takes away the rail, the top bar, the dock, the message box and the page's own row of buttons, and gives the whole display to the page. The same button, now Exit full screen , brings them back, and so does Esc when Daimond has the keyboard. Full screen is never entered on your behalf and is not remembered."},{"p":"capps.html","a":"s7","t":"A worked example","u":"A Diamond&rsquo;s page","b":"One such page ships with Daimond. The button here makes a Diamond called Log Life and puts the page inside it, furnished and ready to record. Daimond asks first, and offers to open the one you have instead of making a second. Make me Log Life Nothing is sent anywhere. It makes one Diamond on this device and copies the page into it. Ask for a variation from inside Log Life itself. Redesign page&hellip; there, and \"the same, but for practice sessions instead of meals\" , gives the daimon a worked example in front of it. A daimon in another Diamond cannot see this page and will start from nothing. &larr; Chats & Diamonds Next: Files, email & the web &rarr;"},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"","t":"Chats & Diamonds","u":"","b":"Chats & Diamonds A chat is a conversation. A Diamond is a saved project with its own memory, its own page and its own files. You keep the one inside the other."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s1","t":"A chat","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"The + beside Chats starts one. Type in the box and send. A new chat is titled New chat until you say something, and after that its first line is its title. While an answer is arriving you can keep typing. A message sent then waits as a dashed bubble and goes as its own turn when the answer finishes. Click it to take it back, or its cross to drop it. Pressing stop hands anything queued back to you, unsent. Deleting a chat moves it to the Trash, so nothing asks first. The two acts that cannot be undone are the ones that ask, and both are in that panel."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s2","t":"Reading a long thread","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"Each turn is a labelled tile, so you can see at a glance who said what. Every turn is a tile, labelled You , Thinking , Tool , Daimond , or as a note from the app itself. Each header carries the local date and time to the minute in the Holocene reckoning, so 12026-09-15 13:12 is this year plus ten thousand. Hover for the full instant with its offset. The header of the chat carries the controls. Show steps shows the work done between your message and the answer. Collapse answers leaves only what you asked. It is the quickest way to skim, and how you start picking turns. Short answers is a standing request for brevity in this chat. It is a file in your workspace that you can edit, not a hidden instruction. The permission chip reads Guarded , Ask every time or Bypass . See Safety & fixes . Two chevrons beside the send button walk back through your own messages and return to the end."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s3","t":"What a stop looks like","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"A turn says how it ended, in exact words. Answered means it finished and said something. Stopped means you pressed stop. Step limit reached means it ran out of rounds. Ended without saying anything means the model returned nothing at all. Ended without an answer, after reasoning means it thought, was asked once to answer or use a tool, and still said nothing. Ended on an error , or Ended: the tool call arrived as text , say what they say. None of these is dressed up as an answer."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s4","t":"Keeping a chat","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"Two buttons sit on a chat's row in the rail. Keep turns the whole conversation into a Diamond. Fold all turns it into a Diamond by summarising it, keeping what was worked out and dropping the back-and-forth. To keep only part, collapse the answers, tick the turns worth keeping, and choose Fold selected . Fold a step you have adopted, and leave a passing remark where it is. Nothing is absorbed on its own. A Diamond's memory changes only through a fold or an edit, and every write is kept in History."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s5","t":"Inside a Diamond","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"Diamonds sit above your chats, and the + makes one. A Diamond has two faces, and a switch in its header moves between them. Crystal is what it knows, and Chat is its own conversation. What this Diamond knows , on the Crystal face. The raw file is one press away. The card lists the Diamond's title and summary in its own words, then everything else it holds, a line each. Edit opens a form. Show raw memory opens the file itself, for when you would rather work in it directly."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s6","t":"The four files a Diamond keeps","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"Memory is kept in the open. Four files sit in the Diamond's own folder, readable in the Workspace panel and by the daimon. crystal.json holds what this Diamond is , meaning its title, summary, sections, facts and links. REQUIREMENTS.md holds what is to do, as objectives with ranked tasks under each. A task leaves it by being ticked or struck out, and by nothing else. DECISIONS.md holds what was ruled, dated, with the reason. New rulings are appended, and old ones are left as they were. STATE.md holds where things are now, and is overwritten each time. All four ride with the daimon on every round inside one budget, so what a Diamond is told about itself is bounded and predictable. A crystal too large to fit whole keeps its overflow readable and searchable instead of dropping it. The counts on the card, Objectives , Open tasks and Done , are read off REQUIREMENTS.md ."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s7","t":"Two kinds of fold","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"The word covers two different acts. Summarise earlier messages , in the chat header, is the app compressing a conversation that no longer fits. Its decisions are filed into DECISIONS.md , its open items into REQUIREMENTS.md , and its next step into STATE.md . What it files can be found afterwards, and what it leaves in a notice cannot. Fold now , on the Crystal face, is a model rewriting what this Diamond remembers from what has happened since. Every write snapshots a version. Filing is repeatable, so running the same fold twice files nothing twice, and the order two folds arrive in does not change what the files end up saying."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s8","t":"History","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"History lists every version, newest first. View reads one, Restore makes it current again, and Delta shows the material a fold was given. Restore keeps what was current first, so a restore can itself be undone."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s9","t":"Acting without being asked","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"A Diamond answers when you prompt it and at no other time, unless you set up a triggered action. Those live under When this Diamond acts on the Crystal face, and Add an action makes one. Two things can set one off, either minutes of your own activity, counted only while you work, or mail arriving in a folder you name. Each action carries an instruction, sent every time, and a context, sent once until you change it. They are an ordinary file, triggers.json , in the Diamond's folder. A new action is stopped , and says so: Stopped. Press &#9654; on the light beside the pulldown to let this action run. One press arms it."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s10","t":"The light","u":"Chats & Diamonds › Acting without being asked","b":"Every action, every Diamond and the Everything row wear the same three-part control, a play, a pause and a round light. The light says whether anything is armed and free to fire. Red. Nothing here will act by itself. Amber. Some of it will, and some is held. Green. Everything set up will act. A fresh account is red everywhere, which is the honest answer to whether anything is running by itself. An action missing its mailbox or its instruction is left out of the count, because it could not fire. Amber can only be arrived at, never set. A held action spends nothing, because it is refused before the turn starts."},{"p":"chats-and-diamonds.html","a":"s11","t":"Tags, links and the Trash","u":"Chats & Diamonds","b":"Tags on the Crystal face files a Diamond in the rail. Up to eight tags, lowercased and deduplicated. Suggested offers four to start from. Tags are inert. They are not sent to a model, they are no part of the memory, and they change nothing an agent does. Filter by tag in the rail narrows by them. Diamonds can also be linked to each other, and the Graph is the picture of that. Deleting a chat or a Diamond trashes it. It is still stored and still synced, out of every daimon's reach, and kept thirty days with the date on the row. Restore brings it back on every device. Delete permanently and Empty trash are the two that ask. &larr; The interface Next: A Diamond&rsquo;s page &rarr;"},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"","t":"Files, email & the web","u":"","b":"Files, email & the web A daimon works from files it can read and write, folders you hand it, mail you can answer, and pages it can show or drive."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s1","t":"The Workspace","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"The Workspace panel shows your files as an ordinary tree. Three chips at the top name where the workspace lives. Browser is this browser's own private storage, reachable by nothing outside Daimond. It is the default, and it syncs to your other devices. Machine is a real folder on this computer's disk. Daimond works in it directly, so the same files are in your file manager. It stays on that computer, and travels only if you share it with the ⇄ control. Chromium-based browsers only. Cloud is where the bytes are kept, which is what lets a workspace be larger than the device holding it. It is not a third place to work. Files are encrypted before they leave. The folder you open as Machine is the whole of what Daimond can see, with nothing above it and nothing beside it."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s2","t":"Getting a file in","u":"Files, email & the web › The Workspace","b":"Upload files , the arrow-into-tray in the header, takes one file or a handful. Import a folder&hellip; copies a folder in, after which it syncs like everything else. Open a real folder with the Machine chip. Nothing is copied, and it syncs only if you share it. Mail arrives here as ordinary files, and a daimon writes here where it is allowed to."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s3","t":"Held, in the cloud, or pinned","u":"Files, email & the web › The Workspace","b":"A file is in one of three states, and the tree says which. Held means it is on this device. In cloud storage means it is only there, marked with a cloud, and clicking it brings it down. Pinned means it is never freed automatically. As storage fills, the files longest unused go back to the cloud on their own. Freeing is not deleting, since the file is still listed and comes back when asked for. A daimon is told plainly when a file it wants is in the cloud, and has to fetch it deliberately. A cap limits how much it may bring down on its own."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s4","t":"Handing a folder over","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"The paperclip on every row reads Attach to current focus . What it does follows what is in focus, whichever row it sits on. A Diamond is in focus. The file or folder joins that Diamond's workspace and stays until you take it out. A chat is in focus. The attachment is for the next turn only. Attaching copies nothing. The file stays where it is and the Diamond points at it, so attaching one folder to two Diamonds still leaves one folder. Each attachment is either Note , meaning the path is worth knowing and costs a few tokens, or Read , meaning the contents are wanted now. Start with Note, which is much the cheaper. Either way the text it generates goes into the message box in front of what you type, where you can edit or delete it. Ticking a folder in the workspace group is the permission, so this chat or Diamond may then read and change what is inside. Ticking a file only puts it in front of the model. A Diamond's Reach row names every folder it may write in, and says so in both trees."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s5","t":"What a daimon can open","u":"Files, email & the web › Handing a folder over","b":"A daimon can open only what is in its Diamond's workspace. Nothing else is readable or writable through the file tools, by the daimon or by any helper it dispatches. The check is in compiled code at the one door every file tool passes, and that code is in a bundle that builds reproducibly from public source, so it is not an instruction in a prompt. That rule settles what a daimon can reach and says nothing about where what it reads ends up, because whatever it opens goes to your model provider with the rest of the turn."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s6","t":"The attachment list","u":"Files, email & the web › Handing a folder over","b":"Rows carry an age, Added {when} . A path that is no longer there is drawn as Gone with no open action, so a dead row never poses as a live one. Anything a daimon attached for itself, such as a fold's harvest or a file an agent kept, sits under one collapsed heading, Added by the daimon , with a count and a Drop all . Your own attachments stay where they are."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s7","t":"Sharing a folder to your other devices","u":"Files, email & the web › Handing a folder over","b":"A Machine folder lives on one computer, and your phone cannot open it. The ⇄ control on the row is how it reaches one anyway, with Share with devices that lack native access . Off, only devices that can open the folder themselves see it. On, it is copied to your other devices, up to 200 MiB per folder. The setting is per folder, whichever device you set it from, and only a device that can open the folder can set it. A folder over the ceiling is left out whole, and a banner says which one and what it weighed. Share a smaller folder, or take some of it out."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s8","t":"Documents, and books","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"Open a .typ file and the Doc panel gains two buttons. Compile lays the document out in the page and writes a preview PDF beside the source, and while those pages are up, every save rebuilds them. Press it on a chapter and it compiles the document that includes the chapter, then takes you to that chapter's page. Both panels have to be on screen for the loop to run. Where the window is too narrow, the Doc panel says so, and one rebuild catches up when they return. The editor need not be Daimond's. A save from any editor writing into the granted folder is picked up within about a second. A phone cannot hold a book's files or afford the memory a layout costs, so it hands the compile to a paired computer that can, sealed to your account exactly as a turn is, and the laid-out pages come back."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s9","t":"Any kind of file","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"Any file is stored and travels, up to a gigabyte each. A document or a spreadsheet opens as a reading view , which shows what it says and not how it prints. Values are as stored, formulas are not recalculated, and macros are neither run nor read. Save a copy writes one to your own device and leaves the file here untouched. Where nothing recognises a file, its bytes are shown, and the panel says so."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s10","t":"Email","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"A browser cannot open a mail connection, so Daimond connects through the gateway, which hands back the raw message and forgets your credential. Your mail is written into the workspace as ordinary files. Add a mailbox with the + in the Email panel. Type your address and Daimond guesses the server settings, and every field stays editable. Most providers want an app password in place of your account password, and the panel says where to find it. The credential is stored on this device under your passphrase, handed to the gateway for one sync, and forgotten. Daimond stores no mail on any server. Only you can send. A daimon can draft a message into the Compose panel. The Send button is the one thing that puts mail on the wire, and only a person can press it. Email is part of Daimond Pro."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s11","t":"The Web panel","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"Ask Daimond to open a page, or paste a link. There are three ways, and the site decides which works. Show it. The page appears in the panel. Most sites refuse to be embedded, so this works for a minority. Read it. Daimond fetches the page and reads its text. It works where showing does not. You do not see the page, and Daimond does. Drive it. With the browser add-on, Daimond opens a real tab, signed in as you, and works it while you watch. It is the only way to work a live, logged-in site. Signing in stays yours. When you sign in on a driven page, Daimond stops watching entirely, taking no text, no picture and no keystrokes, and never sees your password. You hand the wheel back from the browser tab and not from Daimond, so a page cannot take it from you."},{"p":"email-web-files.html","a":"s12","t":"Tools","u":"Files, email & the web","b":"The Tools panel says what Daimond can do. Built-in tools are already yours and free, and the panel lists exactly what is held, so it cannot promise one that is not there. Packs are extras, bought once, in dollars. &larr; A Diamond&rsquo;s page Next: Your computer &rarr;"},{"p":"index.html","a":"","t":"Start here","u":"","b":"Start here Daimond is an AI workspace that runs in your browser, and your chats, keys and files stay on this device. You connect a model, talk to it, and keep what works. No server holds your account. Whatever leaves the browser is named on the page of this guide that covers it. The main ones are a message to the model provider you connected, a page fetched for you, a mailbox synced, and an encrypted parcel sent to a device you linked. First run. The centre lists three steps, and the one you are at is drawn as the button."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s1","t":"Three steps to a first answer","u":"Start here","b":""},{"p":"index.html","a":"s2","t":"Make your account","u":"Start here › Three steps to a first answer","b":"Type a name, and Daimond generates a passphrase of eight random words and shows it once. Write it down. It unlocks everything stored here, and nobody can reset it. Then choose Create account . A password manager is a fine place for it, since eight random words are hard to guess. Under the button, Have a passcode or a pairing code? opens two other routes, redeeming a passcode for an account with credits and pairing this browser to a device you already use. If you already have Daimond elsewhere, pair instead of creating, or you will end up with two accounts."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s3","t":"Connect a model","u":"Start here › Three steps to a first answer","b":"A daimon thinks with a model, so connect one. Press Connect a model in the centre, or open the model row at the foot of the rail. There are three routes. Your own key. Paste a key from Anthropic, Fireworks AI, OpenRouter, Together AI, Groq or DeepInfra. Your messages go straight to that provider, and we charge nothing. Credits. Daimond runs the model for you and draws on a prepaid balance, so there is no provider account to manage. While testing is closed, accounts with credits are given out by passcode. A model of your own. Choose Custom and give the address of any service that speaks the same chat API, including one running on your own machine. Models & providers has the detail."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s4","t":"Say hello","u":"Start here › Three steps to a first answer","b":"Press Say hello , or the + beside Chats , then type in the box and send. The answer arrives in the centre panel, where you will spend most of your time. The chat takes its title from your first line, so a rail of chats reads as a list of subjects."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s5","t":"Then keep what works","u":"Start here","b":"A chat is only a conversation. When one has worked something out, press Keep on its row in the rail and it becomes a Diamond . A Diamond is a saved project with its own memory, page and files. Work inside it starts from what it already knows, so you stop re-explaining yourself. Chats & Diamonds covers it."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s6","t":"What this does that a chat window does not","u":"Start here","b":"It remembers per subject. A Diamond carries what you worked out into every later turn, and you can read and edit that memory as a file. It works on real files. Point it at a folder and it edits the files in place, inside a fence you set. It shows the bill. Each turn is priced on this device as it is spent, from the provider's own figure. It can be checked. The code in this browser is public, builds byte for byte from source, and is sealed into a published record. Verify this build in the About dialog checks the one you are running."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s7","t":"The four words this guide uses","u":"Start here","b":"Diamond , a saved project with its own memory and page. daimon , the assistant that works inside a Diamond. crystal , what a Diamond knows, kept as a file you can read and edit. forge , the shared board where proposals about Daimond itself are filed, shown in the Improve panel."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s8","t":"What is on screen","u":"Start here","b":"The screen has three zones. The rail on the left lists your Diamonds and chats, and under a movable divider shows your account, one status line and what you have spent. The centre holds the conversation, with whatever you are reading beside it. The dock on the right holds what you pull from, such as files, mail, running agents and spending. Across the top, each panel has a chip that opens or closes it. A fresh account shows six, for Diamonds, Chat, Web, Workspace, Tools and Spending, and the rest are one press away in the gallery behind the ⋯ chip. At the far right are the update chip, About, Link another device , this guide and Settings . The interface walks through all of it, and none of it is needed to get an answer."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s9","t":"One status line","u":"Start here","b":"The foot of the rail. One line says where things stand, and Details opens the rest. Above the spend row is one line, which reads Locked , Not signed in , No model connected , Offline , Synced , or This device only . It answers the question most worth asking at that moment, showing the most blocking state first. Press it to open Details , which covers models, tools, permissions, storage, machine, sync and the build you are on. Those are diagnostics, so they stay behind the line until you want them."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s10","t":"Keeping it to hand","u":"Start here","b":"Use your browser's own Install , or Add to Home Screen on a phone, and Daimond gets an icon and a window of its own. It is the same page and the same stored work, so nothing is copied and nothing moves. Daimond updates itself, taking a new build at a quiet moment and not in the middle of a running turn or a half-typed message. The chip beside the top-bar buttons says when one is ready."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s11","t":"Where to go next","u":"Start here","b":""},{"p":"index.html","a":"s12","t":"Chats & Diamonds","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"Reading a long thread, keeping a chat, and what a Diamond remembers."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s13","t":"Files, email & the web","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"Getting files in, sharing a folder to your other devices, mail, and reading a page."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s14","t":"Your computer","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"Letting a daimon edit real folders and run real commands, inside a fence."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s15","t":"Helpers & where turns run","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"Agents dispatched to work in parallel, and naming the machine that picks turns up."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s16","t":"Safety & fixes","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"What Daimond asks before it acts, what stays private, and what to do when something is wrong."},{"p":"index.html","a":"s17","t":"Ask Daimond","u":"Start here › Where to go next","b":"The rail holds a Diamond called Daimond Help . It answers from this build and from this guide, and says which page an answer came from. Next: The interface &rarr;"},{"p":"interface.html","a":"","t":"The interface","u":"","b":"The interface Three zones and a top bar. Once you know the zones, every part of Daimond has a place."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s1","t":"The rail, on the left","u":"The interface","b":"The rail lists what you are working on, with Diamonds at the top and Chats under them, newest first. The + on each head makes a new one, and dragging the divider gives one list more room. Above them is the Everything row, a play, a pause and a round light for the whole app. Pause there and nothing runs anywhere until you release it. Below the divider is your account, showing the name you gave, one status line, and what you have spent today, this week and this month. Start here covers the status line, and Details opens the diagnostics behind it. One of those details is the build you are on. Press it for the release history, which shows what is planned, the build you are running, and every earlier build with its date and its place in a sealed record. You cannot go back to an older build, by design, because an old client holds faults since fixed and may read newer data wrongly."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s2","t":"The centre","u":"The interface","b":"The centre holds the conversation and whatever you are reading beside it. Panels here sit side by side and not over the chat, so you need not leave the conversation to look at something. Width decides how many fit, from two at the narrowest to four at the widest, with one more for roughly every 380 pixels. Narrow the window and the panel opened most recently closes, and the chat is never the one to go. AI holds the conversation, and comes back whenever the centre would otherwise be empty. Web shows a web page, or drives one. Doc shows a file as its characters, with room to read and edit it. Preview shows what a file looks like, whether a picture, a PDF, or typeset pages beside their source. Email message opens one message beside the chat. Tools lists what Daimond can do. Compose is for writing mail, and only you can press Send. Graph draws your Diamonds and the links between them. Terminal is a real terminal on your machine. See Your computer ."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s3","t":"The dock, on the right","u":"The interface","b":"The dock holds what you pull from. Email holds your mailboxes. Workspace shows your files as a tree. Agents lists the helpers running now, with what each is costing. It stays out of the way until the first one runs. Spending shows where the money went. Pending lists what is waiting on you. Each row is answered with Do it , discussed, or dropped. Trash holds deleted chats and Diamonds for thirty days. Social and Improve are covered in Social & proposals ."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s4","t":"The top bar","u":"The interface","b":"The top bar carries the wordmark, a row of chips, and five buttons at the far right, which are the update chip, About, Link another device , this guide, and Settings . Every panel has a chip. Click one to open or close that panel. An open panel's chip is filled in, so the row reads as a map of what is on screen. Chips are grouped by zone in the order the zones sit, and each keeps its position so you can learn it. A fresh account gets six chips, for Diamonds, Chat, Web, Workspace, Tools and Spending. The rest live behind the ⋯ chip at the end of the row. Click it for the gallery, which lists every panel there is, searchable and grouped by zone, with a pin on each row that puts it in the bar. A dock chip is greyed when the dock is full, and its tooltip names the fix. Ctrl K opens any panel or setting by typing."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s5","t":"Settings","u":"The interface","b":"Settings is the last button on the right. It holds how Daimond looks and where things sit, and nothing about what it does. View. Simple shows names only, and Max puts the model, the cost and the context on every tile. A tile's own cog overrides it. Theme. Eleven palettes in three bands. This guide wears whichever the app is wearing. Text size. Four steps. Only the type changes, so for a bigger interface use the browser's zoom. Side panels. How the dock tiles, and so how many panels it holds, whether four in one column, four at 2&times;2, or six at 2&times;3 or 3&times;2. Auto lets the window decide. Language and Currency. Billing is in US dollars whatever the figures show. With a Diamond open, the menu also offers to keep the current arrangement of panels with it, and opening that Diamond again puts the panels back. It is saved only when you ask."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s6","t":"The Graph","u":"The interface","b":"Open Graph to see your Diamonds drawn, with the links between them. Bring the pointer near a tile's edge and four anchors appear. Drag from one and a line follows, snaps to the nearest Diamond, and makes the link when you let go. A link can exist with no relation on it, which means only that the two are joined. Chips offer a Relation on the line that has just appeared, and Esc leaves it blank. Let go over empty canvas and a box asks for a name, then a new Diamond is made there, already linked. Nothing is written until you give the name. Click a link to select it and press Delete to drop it. Its form carries Reverse , which writes a new record, so the note travels and the date does not. Organise lays everything out again to reduce crossings. From the keyboard, select a Diamond, press L , and click the target."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s7","t":"By keyboard, and by ear","u":"The interface","b":"Tab moves through the controls, Enter or Space operates one, and Esc closes what is open. Every dialog, menu and sheet also carries a cross, sized for a thumb. A dialog keeps focus inside itself and hands it back on closing. With a screen reader, an answer is announced once when it arrives instead of being recited as it is written. The window has named regions and real headings throughout. Anything you cannot reach or cannot hear is a fault, so say so on Social ."},{"p":"interface.html","a":"s8","t":"On a phone","u":"The interface","b":"Below the phone width the chips give way to the phone shell. The chat is the floor, and a panel rises over it as a sheet you can drag down again. The rail becomes a drawer, opened at the top left, with your account at the foot of it. The row along the bottom is the same chip row, scrolled sideways, and it carries every panel instead of six. The filled chip is what you are looking at, because on a phone only one thing is, so a chip takes you there instead of toggling. Where a row of controls runs off the edge, it scrolls, and fades at the edge to say so. A Diamond's page has a full-screen button that gives the whole display to the page. Installed to the home screen, Daimond runs in its own window with no browser furniture and holds clear of the notch. It is the same page and the same stored work. &larr; Start here Next: Chats & Diamonds &rarr;"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"","t":"Your computer","u":"","b":"Your computer Daimond can run programs on your computer (a build, a test suite, a linter, a script) inside a compartment the kernel enforces. It is free, it takes two commands at a terminal, and it is Linux only. Read the warning first, before you type either command. A snap or flatpak browser cannot do this, and cannot be made to. The installer detects one and refuses rather than writing into it, so this is the first thing you will meet, and on Ubuntu the default Chromium is a snap. Why, and what to install instead ."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s1","t":"What it gives you","u":"Your computer","b":"With the hand installed, a daimon can run a build, a test suite, a linter or a script against the folder you granted, and read the result. Nothing else on the machine is in reach. The tools it uses are named in the Tools panel, and four of them are worth knowing by name. run runs one command, given as a list of arguments, and answers with the exit code, the tail of the output and the time taken. verify runs your project's own check. Daimond reads the argv from .daimond/verify.json , or infers it from Cargo.toml , package.json , pyproject.toml or go.mod . The exit code is the verdict. runs lists what is still running, and how to stop one. A command can outlive its turn, because something started in the background holds its ports until it is stopped, and nothing else on the computer can reach it, since the compartment scopes signals to itself. serve starts a read-only file server for a folder, so a built page can be looked at in the Web panel. It stays up after the turn, so stop it by its id. A refusal names the path, the cause and the fix, so there is no code to look up. The turn then ends the way any turn does, as Answered , Stopped or Step limit reached , so a refused command neve"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s2","t":"Before you start","u":"Your computer","b":"Three things decide whether any of this will work. Checking them takes a minute, and discovering them afterwards takes an hour, because the error the browser reports names none of them."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s3","t":"1. A browser installed from a .deb","u":"Your computer › Before you start","b":"A snap or flatpak browser confines the programs it starts, and that confinement reaches Daimond's hand. The home interface a snap gets does not grant the hidden directories at the top of your home directory, and the hand keeps its journal at ~/.local/share/daimond/hand/journal/ , behind one of them. The hand cannot open the file it would have recorded its reason in, so it exits without a word, and Chrome reports only Native host has exited . That sentence gives you nothing to act on, and the confinement is what a snap is for. Moving the journal does not help, because the browser hands the program its own environment. You need not work this out yourself. The installer writes into the profiles that can use it and names the ones that cannot, and where every browser it finds is confined it stops, so you are not left with an install that appears to have worked. --check flags a confined browser even with a usable one beside it, because the browser you are actually using decides. To see what you have before you start: snap list 2>/dev/null | grep -Ei 'chrom|brave|vivaldi|edge' flatpak list 2>/dev/null | grep -Ei 'chrom|brave|vivaldi|edge' Anything either command names is confined. What wo"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s4","t":"2. Run that browser once before you install","u":"Your computer › Before you start","b":"The installer writes one small file into the browser's profile directory, and that directory is created the first time the browser starts. Install a browser and never open it, and install.sh reports finding nothing, correctly, because there is nothing there yet."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s5","t":"3. Linux, with Landlock in the kernel","u":"Your computer › Before you start","b":"The compartment is built from Landlock, which fences files, and seccomp, which filters system calls, and both are Linux kernel features. Landlock arrived in Linux 5.13, and Unconventional systems sets out what older and unusual machines get. The hand builds and runs on macOS and Windows, but reports that it can fence nothing, and Daimond then refuses every command instead of running one unfenced. The refusal names what is missing. Neither platform’s fence is built. Installing a different browser? Your account does not come with you. Your account is a signing key held in the browser you made it in. The passphrase does not recreate that key, it only decrypts the copy already stored there, so the same passphrase in a fresh browser starts a different account, with its own credits and no Pro. The key has to travel, so before you retire the old browser do one of these in it: Link another device , then type the code in the new browser; add a passkey; or Export a backup , which writes the chats, Diamonds and workspace files together with the key, still wrapped under your passphrase, for a browser that has no account of its own yet. See More than one device and Safety & fixes . Not the same"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s6","t":"Installing it","u":"Your computer","b":"Installing takes two commands at a terminal, from the top of the Daimond repository, then two things in the browser. They add up to one small JSON file per browser profile plus a binary you built. The install leaves nothing listening on a port, no daemon running, and no secret to steal."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s7","t":"Build the hand","u":"Your computer › Installing it","b":"cargo build --release --manifest-path hand/Cargo.toml Use --manifest-path and not -p , because the hand is a cargo workspace of its own, so -p reports no such package and builds nothing."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s8","t":"Grant a folder, and register the hand","u":"Your computer › Installing it","b":"hand/install/install.sh --workspace ~/work Put your own folder in place of ~/work . That folder bounds everything any command can read or write, so choose one for the work. The script refuses your home directory and / outright, since granting either grants everything. The folder has to exist already. That one run does three things, each with a failure you would otherwise meet later. Writes your folder into root.txt , at ~/.local/share/daimond/hand/journal/root.txt . The hand will not serve a page until it has been told the folder, and it never guesses, because a guessed folder is a guess about what a command may touch. To change it later, run the same command with a different folder. Creates that journal directory at mode 700. It holds the record of every command every Diamond has run, so the hand refuses to write it anywhere other users can read. A directory made under the usual default permissions is 755, and the hand would not start. Writes the registration into every usable browser profile, and prints which. That file is what lets the browser start the hand at all. It is JSON and nothing else, so the script builds nothing, downloads nothing, starts nothing and needs no root. DA"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s9","t":"Load the extension","u":"Your computer › Installing it","b":"The previous step printed the path. Open chrome://extensions , turn on Developer mode , choose Load unpacked , and select the ext/ directory. The extension carries a fixed public key, so its id is the same on every machine, which is how the registration written a moment ago can already name it. Its manifest names a single origin, https://daimond.oxedyne.com , as the only page allowed to speak to it, so no other page in your browser can reach it at all."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s10","t":"Restart the browser","u":"Your computer › Installing it","b":"The browser reads those profile directories only when it starts, so a file that appeared while it was running is invisible until then. A missed restart is the commonest reason a correct install looks broken."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s11","t":"Then check it","u":"Your computer › Installing it","b":"hand/install/install.sh --check The check changes nothing, and prints one line per thing that has to be true: a usable browser, the registration, the binary, the journal directory and its mode, root.txt , the journal sitting outside the granted folder, and the extension. A failing line carries its fix on the line below it. Work from the top, since a later line often fails only because an earlier one did."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s12","t":"The last two steps are yours","u":"Your computer","b":"None of the steps above made a decision for you. The two things left are the two that are decisions. Open the same folder in Daimond. On the Workspace panel, use the Machine chip to open the folder you granted. It has to be the same one. The hand writes a token into <folder>/.daimond/workspace.id and names it when it introduces itself, and the page reads that file through the handle it already holds and compares the two. A workspace that is the browser's own storage, or a different folder from the one in root.txt , has its commands refused with a line saying which. The token lives inside .daimond because the compartment always denies that directory, so a command cannot read the token and cannot answer for a folder it is not in. Allow the first command. The first time Daimond wants to run something, a Daimond Hands window opens and asks. No browser permission covers “may run programs on this computer”, so that window is the approval, and the extension records your answer itself. That is also what makes it revocable, because the browser has nothing to take away. Until you allow it, nothing runs. The window names the folder and the page that asked, and says in one line how far a comma"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s13","t":"When it does not work","u":"Your computer","b":"Start with install.sh --check again. It walks every check here in order and stops at the first thing that is wrong. The three steps it automates are useful on their own, and they are what to reach for when --check passes and Daimond still says the hand is not there."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s14","t":"Run the hand yourself","u":"Your computer › When it does not work","b":"hand/target/release/daimond-hand < /dev/null This starts the hand exactly the way your browser will, with no browser at the other end, so whatever it would have said to the browser it says to you. There are three answers. daimond-hand: the page closed the pipe. The hand is configured and ready. Whatever is wrong is on the browser's side, and restarting it is the first thing to try. This hand has not been told which folder it may work in means root.txt is missing. Run install.sh --workspace again. …is readable by users other than its owner means the journal directory is not 700, and the hand will not write the record of your commands where another account can read it. chmod 700 it. Anything else is a whole sentence naming the path, the cause and the fix, because the hand has nowhere to print a code anyone could look up. hand/target/release/daimond-hand --report This prints what the compartment can enforce on this kernel and, at greater length, what it cannot. Read the second list closely, because on a kernel below Linux 7.1 it includes a route out of the compartment that Landlock leaves open and the hand closes itself, by refusing unix sockets. It also names the folder from root.txt"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s15","t":"Read the journal","u":"Your computer › When it does not work","b":"tail -n 5 ~/.local/share/daimond/hand/journal/hand-*.jsonl The journal holds one JSON object per line, in the order things happened: the handshake, every command, and every refusal with its reason. If the hand started at all it wrote something here. An empty directory after a failed attempt means the hand never got far enough, which on Ubuntu almost always means the confined browser at the top of this page."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s16","t":"Ask the extension","u":"Your computer › When it does not work","b":"In the browser, on the Daimond tab, open the developer console and run: await DaimondHand.status() The extension answers with JSON saying whether a hand is paired, which folder it says it was granted, what it can enforce, and, where the answer is no, a reason written as a whole sentence. Daimond shows the model that same sentence, so the JSON explains what the daimon has been told."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s17","t":"What it actually is","u":"Your computer","b":"A command is an ordinary process, owned by you, on your own kernel, against your own files, with capabilities removed first. There is no virtual machine, no container and no emulation, so nothing is simulated and nothing copied in. Four parts each do one job. The page asks. Daimond works out what this turn may touch and sends the whole rule with the request. It never sends a shell line, because argv , the list of arguments, is an array, and there is nothing to inject into. The extension relays. Daimond Hands is the only thing the page can hand a command to, and one origin may speak to it. A small local program runs it. The hand builds the compartment out of the rule it was sent, applies it to itself, and then becomes the command. No supervisor watches a child, because nothing is left of the hand once the command starts. Your browser starts the hand. That is why there is no port and no password. A service on a port would be reachable by every page you visit. It is free, and it is not part of Pro. Nothing about a command touches Daimond's gateway. The request goes from the page to an extension to a program on your own disk and back, so nothing is metered. A command costs what the mod"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s18","t":"Daimond implements no tools","u":"Your computer › What it actually is","b":"There is no built-in ls , no built-in grep , no bundled toolbox and no shell. ls is /usr/bin/ls , the one already on your machine, at the version you installed. Every program under the system directories is reachable, because the compartment adds them to the grant read-only : /usr , /bin , /sbin , /lib , /lib32 , /lib64 , /libx32 , /etc , /opt , and the harmless devices /dev/null , /dev/zero , /dev/full , /dev/random and /dev/urandom . Without them nothing starts, not even cat , because the loader has to read the shared objects and the process has to open something for the standard streams. A bare name is looked up on PATH , which is /usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin unless a granted toolchain has added to it. What that finds is resolved to an absolute path and checked against the compartment like anything else, so finding is not permission. A PATH pointing outside finds a program the command may not run, and the refusal names it. There is no shell, so there is no shell syntax. A command is a list ( [\"cargo\", \"test\", \"--lib\"] ), and a semicolon, pipe, redirection, backtick, $(…) or && arrives at the program as a literal argument. To chain two commands the daimon runs the tool twice, whi"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s19","t":"What a command can and cannot do","u":"Your computer","b":""},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s20","t":"Files","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"Read and write inside the granted folder. That folder, minus Daimond's own .daimond directory, is the writable world. A private temporary directory. It is writable, with TMPDIR pointing at it, removed when the run ends, unreachable by any other run, and the one place outside your folder a command may write. /tmp is outside the compartment, and a build that cannot write a temporary file fails part-way through for a reason nobody can read. The system, read-only. /usr , /etc , /opt and the rest of the list above. Everything else is refused by the kernel , not by a check somebody wrote. A file one folder outside the grant comes back Permission denied , and the daimon is shown that refusal instead of the file. One consequence is worth knowing before it looks like a broken tool. Where the whole granted folder is writable, that folder itself cannot be listed and a file cannot be created directly in it, because Landlock cannot grant a directory and withhold part of it, so the children are granted one by one. Everything one level down works normally."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s21","t":"Named local sockets are refused, always","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"A command cannot create a unix socket, whatever else it was allowed. That costs a local database over a socket file, docker , anything wanting X11, and ssh-agent . socketpair , the paired socket a build uses to talk to its own child processes, is untouched, so cargo build is unaffected. Below Landlock ABI 9 (Linux 7.1) the compartment does not govern connecting to a socket file at all. We measured it. With the fence in force and the network refused, one message to the desktop's session bus starts a process that was never fenced and reads a file the fence denies. That is a way out of the compartment, and no leak at its edge, so it is closed unconditionally."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s22","t":"ssh does not work","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"Two independent reasons stop it, and closing either alone would not be enough. ~/.ssh is not in the compartment, because every path is built from the folder you granted. And the agent socket is a unix socket, refused above. git push over SSH fails for both reasons at once. Granting your home directory would remove the first reason and most of the point of the compartment, which is why the installer refuses it. Operations that touch only the repository do work. git status , diff , log , add and a local commit are ordinary file operations inside the folder you granted."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s23","t":"A toolchain needs a grant","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"Open a Diamond and the Workspace panel shows a Toolchains row with five buttons: Rust , Node , Python , Go , Git . All five start off, so cargo is refused until you turn Rust on, because ~/.cargo and ~/.rustup sit under your home directory, and your home directory is not in the compartment. Git is the odd one out. git is already in the read-only base, which is why git status and a local commit work with no grant. The button adds your own configuration, read-only, meaning your name, your email, and the hooks core.hooksPath names. Your stored passwords and ~/.ssh are denied by name. Three things about that are deliberate. It is never inferred from what was asked to run. A compartment that widened itself to fit the requested binary would be a compartment the model chooses, and the whole arrangement depends on the compartment being something the model cannot choose. cargo is reachable because you granted the Rust toolchain, and for no other reason. It is per Diamond. A grant belongs to one Diamond, alongside the folders that Diamond holds, and is taken back the same way. It is a short list of named directories, never the home directory. Rust grants ~/.cargo/bin and ~/.rustup read-only,"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s24","t":"The network","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"Whether a command may reach the network depends on the permission mode and on what the turn has already read. Under Ask and Guarded , once a turn has taken in content from outside (a web page it read or fetched, an email, the output of an earlier command), every command in that turn runs with TCP refused, and the daimon is told why, so it reports the cause instead of a broken project. Under Bypass the network is kept whatever the turn has read. A command's own output counts as content from outside. So the first command in a turn has the network and a second one, in the same turn, does not. A build log is written by whatever the build ran, so this is deliberate, and it surprises people. Reading an ordinary file in your own workspace does not count, and a message in your mail does. If something needs to fetch, ask for it in a fresh message, which starts a fresh turn. What is refused is TCP bind and connect, which is what Landlock governs. UDP and raw sockets are outside it."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s25","t":"32-bit binaries are killed","u":"Your computer › What a command can and cannot do","b":"The system-call filter is compiled for one architecture and checks the architecture first, because the compatibility ABI numbers its calls differently, and a filter built for the wrong table would refuse the wrong things and report success. A program that declares a different architecture is therefore killed outright instead of filtered. That fails closed, and a build that execs a 32-bit helper will not work here."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s26","t":"The permission ladder","u":"Your computer","b":"One setting has three rungs, and the axis is what Daimond does without asking . It starts on Guarded."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s27","t":"Ask every time","u":"Your computer › The permission ladder","b":"Every command is put to you before it runs, with the command and the directory on the prompt, and Daimond asks once in each conversation before it reaches the web. Choose it for watching each step, and for a machine holding something you cannot lose."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s28","t":"Guarded","u":"Your computer › The permission ladder","b":"The default. Commands run without asking. A turn that has read outside content loses the network, and Daimond asks once, in that conversation, before reaching anywhere the model chose. One yes covers every site until the conversation ends, a new chat or a fresh daimon asks again, and an unusually long address is always put to you on its own."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s29","t":"Bypass","u":"Your computer › The permission ladder","b":"Nothing is asked, apart from a click an unattended agent wants to make on a page, which is always put to you. Commands run, pages are fetched, and a build needing the network still has it on the turn's tenth command as well as its first. Chosen once, deliberately, then quiet. A rung never changes what is possible. The compartment a command runs inside, the system-call filter under it, the folders a Diamond is scoped to, a granted toolchain, the marking on content from outside, and the journal are identical on all three. A rung changes only what you are asked. The rung is a word in the chat header beside the model, because a mode you have to remember is one you will be wrong about. Click it to change it, or press Ctrl K ( Cmd K on a Mac) and type the rung's name."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s30","t":"Unconventional systems","u":"Your computer","b":"The compartment is built out of a fixed list of absolute paths, and that list decides whether a given machine works. Run --report on yours before assuming either way."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s31","t":"Will not work","u":"Your computer › Unconventional systems","b":"NixOS and Guix. Programs live in /nix/store or /gnu/store , neither of which is granted, and /usr/bin holds almost nothing. A bare name resolves through PATH to a store path outside the compartment, and the command is refused by name. The app cannot widen it today. A kernel without Landlock. That means before Linux 5.13, or compiled out, or absent from the active LSM list, or removed by a hardening patch. The hand reports fence:none and Daimond refuses every command instead of running one unfenced. Check that landlock appears in /sys/kernel/security/lsm . An architecture other than x86-64 or aarch64. The filter's syscall numbers are written out per architecture and there is no third table. The hand will not guess one, so there is no filter, and without a filter no command runs."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s32","t":"Partly","u":"Your computer › Unconventional systems","b":"Linux 5.13 to 6.6. Files are fenced, but Landlock has no network rules before Linux 6.7, so a command that would have to run with the network withheld is refused outright, with a sentence saying so, instead of running with the network it was supposed to lose. Bypass works, and Guarded works until the turn reads something. Homebrew on Linux, Flatpak and Snap. None of /home/linuxbrew/.linuxbrew , the Flatpak trees or /snap is granted, so a program installed that way is refused, while what your distribution put under /usr is reachable in the same session. That concerns the programs a command runs. A browser packaged that way is a separate problem, and a fatal one, covered under Before you start . Version managers other than the two that are known. The toolchain paths are a fixed list, and ~/.nvm and ~/.pyenv are the only version managers on it. asdf , mise , volta , rbenv and sdkman install under ~/.asdf , ~/.local/share , ~/.volta , ~/.rbenv and ~/.sdkman , and nothing grants any of those. The Python grant covers ~/.local/bin and ~/.local/lib and pointedly leaves out ~/.local/share ."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s33","t":"Fine","u":"Your computer › Unconventional systems","b":"Debian, Ubuntu, Fedora, Arch, openSUSE with their own packages, on a kernel new enough for --report to name a Landlock ABI. musl and Alpine , given the same. BusyBox coreutils. A multi-call binary decides what it is from the name it was invoked by, and the hand execs the resolved binary under the name that was asked for , so it still knows which tool it is meant to be."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s34","t":"A terminal, too","u":"Your computer","b":"The Terminal panel opens a real terminal on the same machine, inside the same compartment, and you type into it. It exists because some programs ask their questions of a terminal and not of standard input. Keystrokes are not written to the journal. It needs a hand paired, like everything else here."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s35","t":"What is recorded","u":"Your computer","b":"Every run (what was asked, what it was refused, what it returned) is appended to the journal in ~/.local/share/daimond/hand/journal/ , as ordinary JSON lines you can read. The entries are hash-chained, and the journal sits outside every compartment, because the hand refuses a fence that would reach it. So a command cannot rewrite the record of itself, and no permission mode turns this off."},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s36","t":"What is not protected","u":"Your computer","b":"--report prints two lists, and the second is the reason to run it. Every entry in it was measured on a running kernel and not inferred, and it changes with your kernel, so read your own. The entries fall into a few groups. Asking about a file is not opening it. stat still answers outside the compartment, so sizes, timestamps, ownership and the presence or absence of a file are readable. The compartment governs opening. The system grant is wide. /usr , /etc and /opt read-only is what lets a command run at all, and it also means every world-readable file under /etc can be read and every tool on the machine executed. /etc in particular holds a great deal of machine-identifying detail. The filter is a deny-list. It removes named capabilities from a command and is not a syscall sandbox. Anything it did not name is permitted, including a call a future kernel adds. A filter cannot follow a pointer. It sees a syscall number and six registers, never what they point at, so it sees no path, no address and no filename. A refused chmod is therefore refused everywhere and an allowed one allowed everywhere. chmod 777 is refused, and chmod 644 on a private key is not, because 644 adds no write and"},{"p":"machine-operations.html","a":"s37","t":"Taking it back","u":"Your computer","b":"Three different things can be taken back, and all three come up. Withdraw the permission. Click the Daimond Hands icon in the toolbar. Running commands on this computer is listed there beside the sites you have approved, with a Revoke button. Revoking stops whatever is running at that moment, immediately, and does not wait for the end of the current build. Take the folder away. Delete root.txt . The hand then refuses to serve at all, whatever the browser says, and the refusal is a whole sentence. Remove the registration. Run hand/install/uninstall.sh , or with --dir DIR for one directory. That deletes the file the installer wrote, so the browser can no longer start the hand. The binary is left where it is, because the script did not put it there. &larr; Files, email & the web Next: Helpers & where turns run &rarr;"},{"p":"models.html","a":"","t":"Models & providers","u":"","b":"Models & providers A daimon thinks with a model. Bring a key you already hold and pay that provider directly, or buy credits and let Daimond run one for you."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s1","t":"Your own key","u":"Models & providers","b":"Open the model row at the foot of the rail. It lists a key for each provider you use and which model new work starts on. + Add provider asks the same three questions whoever the provider is. Choose the provider, paste the key, star a default model, then save."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s2","t":"Choose a provider","u":"Models & providers › Your own key","b":"Anthropic, Fireworks AI, OpenRouter, Together AI, Groq or DeepInfra. Custom reveals a Base URL box for any other service speaking the same chat API, including one on your own machine."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s3","t":"Paste the key","u":"Models & providers › Your own key","b":"It is stored wrapped under your passphrase, and sent to the provider you chose and to nobody else. It reaches your other devices only inside the encrypted sync parcel, still wrapped."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s4","t":"Star a default model","u":"Models & providers › Your own key","b":"List models fetches the catalogue where the provider offers one, and otherwise you type the model id. The starred one is where new chats and Diamonds begin. Your messages then go straight to that provider on your own account. Daimond is out of the path, and we charge nothing."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s5","t":"Choosing a different model","u":"Models & providers","b":"The default is a starting point. Any connected model can be chosen for one chat from the selector in its header, and the default is untouched. A Diamond can also carry a separate Helper model for the agents it dispatches, which applies to the next one dispatched and to none already running. Where a model publishes no context window, the tile says Context size unknown for this model. and guesses nothing."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s6","t":"Choosing who serves it","u":"Models & providers","b":"A router hands one model to whichever upstream provider it likes, and they are not equally good. Under an OpenRouter provider, each model row carries a Providers field, with the placeholder prefer, -avoid, ! . A bare name prefers that provider. A leading - avoids one. A bare ! means only these, refusing every fallback. So Novita, -DeepInfra prefers one and avoids another. The field is saved as you leave it. A provider whose catalogue has not been fetched keeps its rows behind a Models&hellip; disclosure, so a preference already set stays reachable."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s7","t":"Credits","u":"Models & providers","b":"Credits run the model for you with no key needed, and they also meter the few things that leave the browser: fetching a page, the bandwidth of mail and of cross-device sync, and cloud storage beyond the free tier. Sync, Email and cloud storage themselves are turned on by Daimond Pro, and metered use is paid from credits with or without it. Open the credits row for your balance and the packs. Credits are not a subscription, and you keep what you buy. Payment happens on the payment provider's own hosted page, so no card number is typed into Daimond. While testing is closed, new accounts with credits are given out by passcode. Daimond works with your own provider key without one."},{"p":"models.html","a":"s8","t":"Auto-reload","u":"Models & providers","b":"Auto-reload is a standing instruction to buy credits when they run low, so a long job does not stop halfway. It is the one place Daimond may charge a saved card while you are away, so it is deliberately explicit. Three numbers set it. Below is the balance at which a top-up fires. Buy is how much one top-up adds. Never spend more, per month, than is a hard cap on the calendar month. Set this one carefully. It is enforced on the same code path as the charge. It cannot be switched on without a saved card, because a control that turned on and then failed silently would be worse than one that is disabled and says why. &larr; Social & proposals Next: Spending &rarr;"},{"p":"runner.html","a":"","t":"Helpers & where turns run","u":"","b":"Helpers & where turns run A daimon can put helpers to work in parallel, and a turn you start on one device can be run by another. Both are visible, and both are yours to stop."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s1","t":"Helpers","u":"Helpers & where turns run","b":"Some tasks want work done, and for those a Diamond's daimon dispatches helpers , agents that run on their own with the file tools, each given the standing instructions and this Diamond's memory. A helper cannot see your conversation. The memory is the whole of what it knows about the work, so the task it is handed has to say everything else. Up to eight run at once and the rest queue. The daimon dispatches them and its turn ends there. Each reports back, and you keep the ones worth keeping. A helper cannot dispatch another helper, so delegation goes one level and stops. A Diamond's settings carry a Helper model , separate from the one its daimon thinks with. A helper keeps the model it started on, and a change applies to the next one."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s2","t":"The Agents panel","u":"Helpers & where turns run › Helpers","b":"Live runs appear in Agents , in the dock, with a line saying how many are running, paused and queued. Each tile shows its cost as it accrues, and not only at the end. Three controls sit in the header and again on every tile. Pause. Hang up what is running and hold the queue. A running helper keeps what it had produced, and nothing new starts. Resume. Carry on from where each left off, without starting over, so a pause costs nothing but the wait. Stop. End them outright. Each keeps what it managed. Clear finished agents tidies up. The wider brake is the Everything row at the top of the rail, which covers every Diamond, every chat, the helpers, the mailboxes, and any page being fetched. A paused thing refuses a turn before it starts, so nothing is spent."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s3","t":"Which device runs a turn","u":"Helpers & where turns run","b":"With more than one device on your account, one can run a turn for another and the answer syncs back. Hand-off is off until you turn it on in the permission chip, with Hand off when you step away . It helps most on a phone, which cannot run a command on your computer, and on a laptop you are about to close."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s4","t":"Naming the device that picks turns up","u":"Helpers & where turns run › Which device runs a turn","b":"Without a nomination, a handed-off turn goes to whichever device is freshest and awake. The freshest device is usually the right one, but a laptop about to close can take a turn and take it with it. Open Settings and then Devices . Each row ends in a star. Press the hollow one beside the machine that is usually on and it fills in. There is exactly one at a time, so starring a second clears the first. The nomination travels with your ordinary sync. The nomination is only half of it. The next time that machine is running Daimond it asks Make this device the runner? Answer This device is the runner and it holds that posture from then on. The posture does not travel, because it commits that machine to staying awake and listening. The question is asked once. Say no and it is not asked again until you clear the nomination and make it afresh."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s5","t":"Keeping the browser out of the way","u":"Helpers & where turns run › Which device runs a turn","b":"A browser slows a hidden tab to about one wake-up a minute and may park a whole page to save memory. A slowed device is worse than none, because it still reports itself awake and collects nothing. So give it a browser of its own. dev/runner-chrome.sh in the Daimond source starts one with a separate profile, an app window in place of a tab, and those throttles off. Run it with --dry-run to read the command first, or --install to have it start with your desktop session. Two things are beyond the script. Turn Memory Saver off in that profile, or add Daimond to its exceptions. And leave the window open and not minimised, because behind other windows is fine and minimised is treated as hidden."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s6","t":"What you see while it runs","u":"Helpers & where turns run › Which device runs a turn","b":"The turn says where it went. First it reads Sent to your other devices , then the machine's own name as it picks the turn up and while it works. The answer appears where you started it. Two buttons sit beside that line the whole time. Take back returns the turn to the device in your hand, and Run here runs it locally if nothing picked it up. Naming your devices under Settings &rsaquo; Devices is what makes these lines read as a name. A turn handed off looks for the nominated device first, then any other awake computer that is listening, then the device you are on. A phone is never given another device's turn."},{"p":"runner.html","a":"s7","t":"Two things that still stop it","u":"Helpers & where turns run › Which device runs a turn","b":"Both need a moment of your attention on that machine, and neither shows up on the device you are holding. A folder permission waiting for a click. If a turn needs a folder the browser has not granted, the request is raised on that machine's own screen and waits. Open the folder there once and later turns find the permission given. A locked screen. A computer keeps the key it holds for that tab across a reload, so an update no longer stops it. You can turn that off per device with Stay unlocked across reloads under Devices . A phone is never kept unlocked, so a phone doing this work comes back locked. If a device has stopped taking turns and nothing else explains it, go and look at its screen. &larr; Your computer Next: More than one device &rarr;"},{"p":"social.html","a":"","t":"Social & proposals","u":"","b":"Social & proposals Two panels and one Diamond. Social is where you tell us something. Improve is the board it arrives on. Pending is where anything waiting on you turns up."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s1","t":"Saying something","u":"Social & proposals","b":"Open Social and write. The first line is the title, and what happened goes under it. Send sends what is on screen and the build id, and nothing else goes with it. Posting needs a posting name , which the app calls your voice. Without one you can read everything and keep a note to this device. The panel says so in one row, Set a posting name to send. , with Get one beside it. It takes one tap, and it is part of Daimond Pro."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s2","t":"A note that can be acted on","u":"Social & proposals › Saying something","b":"A note needs three things, in this order. Leave any of them out and the note needs a conversation before anything can be done. Where it is. Which panel, and which part of it. What you expected. What happened instead. The build id is attached for you, so you need not find it."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s3","t":"Naming where","u":"Social & proposals › Saying something","b":"There are four regions, and they are the ones this guide uses throughout: the top bar , the rail on the left, the centre , and the dock on the right. On a phone, the drawer is the rail and a sheet is a dock panel. Inside a region, these are the words worth knowing. chip , the small pill in the top bar. tile , a Diamond, a chat or an agent in a list. head , a section's heading row. cog , the settings for the thing it sits on. light , the round one beside a play and a pause. composer , the message box and its buttons. row and dialog , which mean what they say. \"The cog on the Life log tile\" is unambiguous, and \"the settings button\" is not."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s4","t":"The board","u":"Social & proposals","b":"Improve shows what has been proposed and what is being done about it, in four columns: Awaiting you , Greenlit (accepted, not built yet), Shipped , and Dropped . Reading needs nothing. Voting and commenting need a posting name. Only we can add or move a card, and the board says nothing about who decides. If it cannot be reached it says Could not load. and nothing more, because that is all it knows."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s5","t":"Pending","u":"Social & proposals","b":"Pending , in the dock, holds what is waiting on you, which is a daimon asking permission for something, and proposals about your own account. Sort by priority, newest or oldest. Each row is answered one of three ways, Do it , Discuss it first , or Drop it . Empty, it says Nothing waiting on you."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s6","t":"The Optimiser","u":"Social & proposals","b":"Your rail holds a Diamond called Daimond Optimiser . It finds what should have been written down, what should have been cheaper and what should have been automatic, and hands you the change to make. It reads counts kept on this device, meaning turns, spend, models and tool calls, and nothing else. It does not see what any conversation was about. It ships stopped, and only you can start it. Open its settings with the cog on its tile, find the action After 30 minutes of your activity , and press play on the light beside it. After that it reads the counts every half hour you actually work. What it finds arrives as a tile in Pending , wherever you happen to be, with a headline, the evidence under it, and Do it . The note on the tile says where it came from, Found by its Diamond. &ldquo;Do it&rdquo; asks that Diamond to carry it out. Nothing is held open by one, so the tick is a fresh instruction, not a permission you gave earlier. The Optimiser alone is denied two things, because it runs while nobody is looking. It cannot publish in your name, and it cannot dispatch helpers."},{"p":"social.html","a":"s7","t":"People, messages and sharing","u":"Social & proposals","b":"People connects you to somebody directly. Show your code to them in the room, or read theirs. From there you have private messages between the two of you, and groups. A group can be closed by whoever made it, and then nothing already said is taken away and nothing new arrives. A Diamond can be shared with somebody you are connected to. The shared Diamond carries its memory and its page, and both are sealed to the people you sent it to. Each of these depends on the relay being switched on. Where it is not, the panel says so in one line, and offers no control that would fail. &larr; More than one device Next: Models & providers &rarr;"},{"p":"spending.html","a":"","t":"Spending","u":"","b":"Spending Model calls are priced on this device, as they happen, from the provider's own figure. The bill arrives in front of you as it is spent, wherever the provider reports a figure, and not later as a surprise."},{"p":"spending.html","a":"s1","t":"Two pots, kept apart","u":"Spending","b":"The two pots are different accounts in different currencies, so they are shown as two tracks and not added together. Inference covers the model calls billed to your key . They are recorded here per turn, and none of them passes through us. Credits holds the prepaid balance, spent on the model when Daimond runs it for you and on what leaves the browser, web, mail, sync , and cloud storage. The foot of the rail carries the running figures for today, this week and this month. Press one to open the Spending panel, which shows a headline figure per pot, a daily graph, a breakdown by category, and a plain table of movements."},{"p":"spending.html","a":"s2","t":"What stops a runaway","u":"Spending","b":"A workspace that runs many agents at once can spend quickly, and the danger lies in a sudden rate more than in a large monthly total. Four measures catch the rate, and none asks you to set a limit in advance. A pace check before a fan-out. The cost of dispatching a batch is known before any of it starts. A batch that would run faster than your own recent pace is held, and Daimond asks once, with the number on the prompt. A few helpers of ordinary cost do not trip it, and a sudden fifty do. Pause and stop, always to hand. The Agents panel pauses everything running, or stops it. The Everything row at the top of the rail pauses the whole app. A paused thing refuses a turn before it starts, so a look costs nothing. A separate key for every agent. Each helper running on credits spends its own freshly minted key, capped against what is left after its siblings' claims. No two share a key, so parallel helpers cannot race one allowance past its cap. Everything metered, per turn, on your device. Every call is priced and recorded as it happens, and the graphs are drawn from that record."},{"p":"spending.html","a":"s3","t":"A ceiling you set","u":"Spending","b":"Settings carries a per-turn spend cap. A turn that reaches the round limit continues on its own, so this cap is the ceiling that holds a runaway. It applies where the provider reports what a round cost, and only there."},{"p":"spending.html","a":"s4","t":"Watching one agent","u":"Spending","b":"Each tile in Agents shows its cost as it accrues, in tokens and in money, and not only at the end. Under the header a line says how many are running, paused and queued, which makes the size of a fan-out legible before you decide whether to hold it. The aim is a team of agents you can run without fear that a loop, or a fan-out larger than you realised, can cost more than a glance and a click can undo. &larr; Models & providers Next: Safety & fixes &rarr;"},{"p":"sync.html","a":"","t":"More than one device","u":"","b":"More than one device Daimond keeps your work on the device you are using, so a second device starts empty. Link it, and your chats, Diamonds and files stay in step without the server ever reading them. What crosses is one encrypted parcel, and only your devices hold the key to it. The key comes from your passphrase and does not leave the browser."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s1","t":"Link a second device","u":"More than one device","b":"Each device makes its own identity, so typing your passphrase into a fresh browser starts a separate account. To use the one you have, link the new device to it."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s2","t":"On the device you already use, open the link dialog","u":"More than one device › Link a second device","b":"Link another device in the top bar shows a QR code and a one-time pairing code. Your first device shows a QR and a code."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s3","t":"On the new device, scan the QR or type the code","u":"More than one device › Link a second device","b":"Point the phone's camera at the code. It opens Daimond and fills the code in, so you only tap Link this device . Without a camera, open Daimond there, choose Have a passcode or a pairing code? , and type it. After scanning, one tap."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s4","t":"Unlock with the same passphrase","u":"More than one device › Link a second device","b":"The new device now holds your account. Use the passphrase you already have, and your work appears. A passkey can unlock it faster afterwards. If a device asks you to create a passphrase instead of entering one, it is about to start a new account. Go back and pair it, or import a backup. One account, one passphrase, on every device you link. The passphrase cannot travel on its own, because it only decrypts a key the device already holds, so a browser that has never been linked has nothing for it to open."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s5","t":"What travels","u":"More than one device","b":"Your chats, your Diamonds and the way you laid them out, your mail, your workspace files, the devices on the account, and what you have paused all travel. Any kind of file travels, up to a gigabyte each. So does the Trash, so a deletion and a restore both reach every device. What syncs is also what is kept in cloud storage, so a workspace can be larger than the device reading it. A file that is not here still shows in the tree and comes down when asked for. A folder you opened on one computer is not in the browser's storage, so it does not travel by default. Files, email & the web covers the per-folder control that copies one to devices that cannot open it."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s6","t":"Running a turn somewhere else","u":"More than one device","b":"When more than one of your devices is awake, one can run a turn for another and the answer syncs back. One switch turns it on, Hand off when you step away . A turn on this computer then moves to another awake device when you close it, whether the lid shuts, the tab closes or the connection drops, and finishes there. Helpers & where turns run covers naming the machine that should pick turns up, and the two things that stop it. An errand sent to another device, and the answer it sends back, are sealed to your account like everything else. The server routes a parcel it cannot open."},{"p":"sync.html","a":"s7","t":"What it costs","u":"More than one device","b":"Cross-device sync is part of Daimond Pro , at $8 a month, billed monthly, and you can cancel any time. Pro also turns on cloud storage, Email and a posting name. Away for a month, the billing pauses on its own and resumes when you are back. Without Pro a device works on its own and loses nothing. Inference, bandwidth and other metered use are paid from credits either way. &larr; Helpers & where turns run Next: Social & proposals &rarr;"}],"alias":{"password":["passphrase"],"passwd":["passphrase"],"login":["sign in","unlock"],"signin":["sign in","unlock"],"byok":["own key","provider key"],"apikey":["provider key"],"api":["provider key"],"key":["provider key"],"cost":["spend","credits","price"],"price":["credits","spend"],"billing":["credits","spend"],"money":["credits","spend"],"iphone":["phone","device"],"android":["phone","device"],"ios":["phone","device"],"mobile":["phone"],"darkmode":["theme","palette"],"colour":["theme","palette"],"color":["theme","palette"],"font":["text size","theme"],"shortcut":["keyboard"],"hotkey":["keyboard"],"backup":["export"],"restore":["import","backup"],"delete":["remove"],"erase":["remove","delete"],"gpt":["model"],"claude":["model"],"openai":["provider"],"anthropic":["provider"],"agent":["worker","agents"],"bot":["daimon","agent"],"terminal":["machine","run"],"shell":["machine","run"],"offline":["sync","connection"]}};
